---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `local name = "Ada"
local age = 17
local onDuty = true
local assignment = nil

print(name, age, onDuty, assignment)`;
const Figure1Out = `Ada\t17\ttrue\tnil`;

const Figure2 = `print(type("SKYNEST"))
print(type(17))
print(type(true))
print(type(nil))
print(type(print))`;
const Figure2Out = `string
number
boolean
nil
function`;

const Figure3 = `local reading = 42
print(reading, type(reading))

reading = "offline"
print(reading, type(reading))`;
const Figure3Out = `42\tnumber
offline\tstring`;

const Figure4 = `do
  shieldLevel = 100
end

print(shieldLevel)`;
const Figure4Out = `100`;

const Figure5 = `do
  local shieldLevel = 100
end

print(shieldLevel)`;
const Figure5Out = `nil`;

const Figure6 = `local playerName = "Ada"

print(playerNmae)`;
const Figure6Out = `nil`;

const Figure7 = `local a, b = 1, 2
print(a, b)

a, b = b, a
print(a, b)`;
const Figure7Out = `1\t2
2\t1`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua - Skynest status board

local shipName = "Skynest"
local crew = 4
local online = true

print(shipName, crew, online)`;
const Task1Out = `Skynest\t4\ttrue`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua - Skynest status board

local shipName = "Skynest"
local crew = 4
local online = true
local nextPort = nil

print(shipName, crew, online, nextPort)
print(type(shipName), type(crew), type(online), type(nextPort))`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua - Skynest status board

local shipName = "Skynest"
local crew = 4
local online = true
local nextPort = nil

print(shipName, crew, online, nextPort)
print(type(shipName), type(crew), type(online), type(nextPort))

print(shipNmae)`;

const Checkpoint = `Skynest\t4\ttrue\tnil
string\tnumber\tboolean\tnil
nil`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.2: Variables, local, and nil</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Lua has no <CodeSnippet
              code="let"
              lang={"js"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, no{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="const"
              lang={"js"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, and no type names to write. There is exactly one keyword you
            need, and it is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            The value decides the type, and you can ask what a value is with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="type()"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Notice the last one. <b class="text-sn-theme">A function is a
            value</b>, the same as a number is. That fact does almost nothing
            for you today and becomes the most useful thing you know by
            Activity 1.8.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing pins a variable to the type it started with, either.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure3Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <h3 class="mt-5">Why <code>local</code> is not optional</h3>

          <p>
            Here is the part of Lua most worth getting right on day one.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">If you leave off{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, the variable is global</b> &mdash; visible to every line of
            every file in your program, for the rest of its run.
          </p>

          <p>
            A{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="do ... end"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            block is a chunk of code with its own scope. Watch what leaks out of
            it and what does not.
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="code"
                lang="lua"
                title="Without local"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="mb-3"
              />
              <Code
                code={Figure4Out}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Output"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={Figure5}
                frame="code"
                lang="lua"
                title="With local"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="mb-3"
              />
              <Code
                code={Figure5Out}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Output"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4">
            Globals are slower, they collide with each other, and they make a
            bug in one file able to break a different one. Lua will never warn
            you. <b>Write{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            every time until leaving it off feels wrong.</b>
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">nil, and the trap that comes with it</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is Lua's word for <i>nothing here</i>. It is a real value with its
            own type, and it is what you get from any variable that was never
            given anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which produces the single most common Lua bug there is:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure6}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure6Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">That typo is not an error.</b>{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="playerNmae"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is simply a global nobody ever assigned, so it is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, and Lua prints it without complaint. Your program runs, it is
            just wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            When something in Lua is mysteriously{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, <b>check your spelling before you check your logic.</b> It is
            the typo far more often than it is the reasoning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Several at once</h3>

          <p>
            Lua lets you assign a whole row of variables on one line, and the
            right-hand side is worked out before anything is stored. That gives
            you a swap with no temporary variable.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure7}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure7Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Open your <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b> and replace what is
            in it. You are turning last activity's hard-coded readout into one
            driven by variables.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Store the readings</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Declare three <CodeSnippet
                code="local"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              variables: a name, a crew count, and whether the ship is online.
            </li>
            <li>Use a string, a number, and a boolean &mdash; one of each.</li>
            <li>Print all three with a single print call.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={Task1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Add something you do not know yet</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a variable for the next port and set it to{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="nil"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />. The ship has no orders yet, and that is information worth
              storing.
            </li>
            <li>Print it alongside the others.</li>
            <li>
              Add a second print that reports the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="type()"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              of all four.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "6, 8-9" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Break it on purpose</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a line that prints{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="shipNmae"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              &mdash; the misspelling is the point.
            </li>
            <li>
              Run it. <b>Note that you get no error</b>, and write down in a
              comment what you got instead.
            </li>
            <li>
              This is the failure you will spend the most time hunting later.
              Meet it now, while you already know the answer.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "11" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Swap the values of two of your variables on one line, with no
              third variable, and print them before and after.
            </li>
            <li>
              Delete the word{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="local"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              from one declaration. Confirm that the program still runs
              identically &mdash; then explain to yourself, in a comment, why
              that is a bad thing rather than a convenience.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>Your finished file should produce this.</p>

          <Code
            code={Checkpoint}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Three things to be able to say out loud before moving on: what{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            does, what{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            means, and why a misspelled variable name does not stop the program.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a class="btn btn-outline-light" href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-1">
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-3">
            Next: Working with Strings <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
